# Curated tactical positions. Each annotation records the intended motif and why the accepted move fits it.
{"id":"mate-in-one-queen-mate-001","motif":"mating_net","fen":"7k/5Q2/5K2/8/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1","acceptable_moves":["f7g7"],"annotation":"Qg7 is checkmate: the queen checks the h8 king, the white king protects g7, and the queen controls the king's remaining escape squares.","phase":"endgame"}
{"id":"knight-fork-king-queen-001","motif":"fork","fen":"5k2/8/5q2/2N5/8/8/8/7K w - - 0 1","acceptable_moves":["c5d7"],"annotation":"Nd7 attacks the black king on f8 and the queen on f6 at the same time.","phase":"middlegame"}
{"id":"undefended-queen-rook-capture-001","motif":"hanging_piece","fen":"q6k/8/8/8/8/8/7K/R7 w - - 0 1","acceptable_moves":["a1a8"],"annotation":"The rook captures the undefended queen on a8; the resulting rook also checks the king on h8.","phase":"middlegame"}
{"id":"black-knight-checking-fork-king-queen-001","motif":"fork","fen":"7k/8/2n5/8/8/5K2/4Q3/8 b - - 0 1","acceptable_moves":["c6d4"],"annotation":"Nd4 checks the white king on f3 while attacking the queen on e2, creating a checking fork.","phase":"middlegame"}
{"id":"rook-captures-pinned-rook-001","motif":"pin","fen":"4k3/4r3/8/8/1B6/8/8/K3R3 w - - 0 1","acceptable_moves":["e1e7"],"annotation":"The black rook on e7 is absolutely pinned to the king on e8 by the white rook on e1; Rxe7+ wins the pinned rook, which is protected by the bishop on b4.","phase":"endgame"}
{"id":"white-rook-skewers-king-and-queen-001","motif":"skewer","fen":"q7/8/k7/8/8/8/7K/1R6 w - - 0 1","acceptable_moves":["b1a1"],"annotation":"Ra1+ places the black king on a6 in check along the a-file with the black queen on a8 behind it. When the king leaves the line, the rook attacks the queen; the queen cannot safely remain on the file.","phase":"endgame"}
{"id":"black-rook-skewers-king-and-queen-001","motif":"skewer","fen":"1r6/7k/8/8/8/K7/8/Q7 b - - 0 1","acceptable_moves":["b8a8"],"annotation":"Ra8+ checks the white king on a3 along the a-file with the white queen on a1 behind it. The king must answer the check, exposing the queen to the rook.","phase":"endgame"}
{"id":"black-knight-captures-hanging-rook-001","motif":"hanging_piece","fen":"7k/8/2n5/4R3/8/8/8/K7 b - - 0 1","acceptable_moves":["c6e5"],"annotation":"Nxe5 captures the undefended white rook on e5. The rook is attacked by the knight and has no supporting white piece.","phase":"middlegame"}
{"id":"black-rook-captures-absolutely-pinned-rook-001","motif":"pin","fen":"k3r3/8/8/1b6/8/8/4R3/4K3 b - - 0 1","acceptable_moves":["e8e2"],"annotation":"Rxe2+ captures the white rook absolutely pinned to the white king on e1 by the black rook on e8. The bishop on b5 protects e2, so the king cannot recapture the rook.","phase":"endgame"}
{"id":"white-knight-discovered-check-queen-attack-001","motif":"discovered_attack","fen":"4k3/7q/8/8/4N3/8/8/K3R3 w - - 0 1","acceptable_moves":["e4f6"],"annotation":"Nf6+ moves the knight off the e-file, uncovering the rook's check against the black king on e8. The knight simultaneously attacks the black queen on h7, so Black must answer the discovered check while the queen is threatened.","phase":"middlegame"}
{"id":"black-knight-captures-checking-rook-001","motif":"defensive_resource","fen":"4k3/8/8/8/8/8/2n5/4R2K b - - 0 1","acceptable_moves":["c2e1"],"annotation":"Nxe1 captures the rook that is checking the black king along the e-file. The knight's capture removes the checking piece immediately and resolves the check.","phase":"middlegame"}
{"id":"white-pawn-promotes-with-check-001","motif":"endgame_tactic","fen":"2k5/P7/1K6/8/8/8/8/8 w - - 0 1","acceptable_moves":["a7a8q"],"annotation":"a8=Q+ promotes the passed pawn with check. The new queen checks the black king on c8 along the eighth rank, gaining a tempo while converting the pawn into a queen.","phase":"endgame"}
{"id":"black-pawn-promotes-with-check-001","motif":"endgame_tactic","fen":"8/8/8/8/8/2K5/p7/2k5 b - - 0 1","acceptable_moves":["a2a1q"],"annotation":"a1=Q+ promotes the passed pawn with check against the white king on c3 along the a1-b2-c3 diagonal. Promotion with check prevents White from using a free tempo to stop the pawn.","phase":"endgame"}
{"id":"white-knight-double-attack-queen-rook-001","motif":"double_attack","fen":"5r1k/8/5q2/2N5/8/8/8/7K w - - 0 1","acceptable_moves":["c5d7"],"annotation":"Nd7 attacks the black queen on f6 and rook on f8 simultaneously, creating a non-checking double attack.","phase":"middlegame"}
{"id":"black-bishop-deflects-queen-from-mating-square-001","motif":"deflection","fen":"5r1k/p2B2pp/4R3/8/2Q5/P4q2/7P/b5K1 b - - 1 1","acceptable_moves":["a1d4"],"annotation":"...Bd4+ forces the white queen on c4 to capture the checking bishop or answer the check another way; after 2.Qxd4, the queen has been drawn away from its defence of f1 and ...Qf1# is checkmate. The bishop sacrifice deflects the queen from the mating square.","phase":"middlegame"}

{"id":"white-queen-decoy-draws-king-into-rook-mate-001","motif":"decoy","fen":"r1b2r1k/1pppqp1p/8/p1bR4/2P5/PP2PP2/2Q2P1P/4KBR1 w - - 0 1","acceptable_moves":["c2h7"],"annotation":"Qxh7+ offers the queen with check and lures the black king from h8 onto h7. After ...Kxh7, Rh5# uses the rook to deliver checkmate. The queen sacrifice is a decoy that attracts the king onto the mating square.","phase":"middlegame"}
{"id":"black-rook-removes-c4-pawn-defender-before-knight-mate-001","motif":"removal_of_defender","fen":"2r5/p6p/1p1n1k2/4r1p1/2PKp1PP/4B3/P1R2P2/2R5 b - - 0 1","acceptable_moves":["c8c4"],"annotation":"...Rxc4+ sacrifices the rook to remove the white c4-pawn, which is the defender of b5. After 2.Rxc4, ...Nb5# delivers checkmate because the king on d4 is exposed to the knight and the c4-pawn no longer controls b5.","phase":"middlegame"}
{"id":"white-rook-interference-diverts-knight-from-queen-defence-001","motif":"interference","fen":"k3q2r/pp4p1/4n3/6Bp/7P/P5P1/1PQ2PK1/3R4 w - - 0 1","acceptable_moves":["d1d8"],"annotation":"1.Rd8+! is an interference sacrifice on the eighth rank. If 1...Nxd8, the knight blocks Black queen e8 from defending the back rank and 2.Qc8# mates the king on a8. If 1...Qxd8, 2.Bxd8 wins the queen because the bishop on g5 protects d8. The position and main variation are reproduced from Ilya Korzhenevich, Illinois Chess Bulletin, Sep–Oct 2005, p.22.","phase":"middlegame"}
{"id":"black-rook-interference-diverts-rook-from-queen-defence-001","motif":"interference","fen":"3r4/6pk/6pp/p7/1q6/5B2/1Q3PPP/1R4K1 b - - 0 1","acceptable_moves":["d8d1"],"annotation":"...Rd1+! interferes with White\u0027s defensive coordination. If 2.Rxd1, ...Qxb2 wins White\u0027s queen because the rook has been drawn away from b1. If 2.Bxd1, ...Qe1# mates: the bishop on d1 blocks White\u0027s rook from reaching e1 and the queen checks along the first rank. This is the Black-to-move interference-and-mate example in Cor van Wijgerden, Step 4, reminder 4-6, position 6.","phase":"middlegame"}
